<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch05_P05_Ex6_one_more_or_less</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The exercise above claims that for every integer \(n\) with \(1 \le n \le 4\), the number \(n^3 + 7\) is 1 more or 1 less than a multiple of 7.</p>
<ol type="a">
<li><p>For which of these \(n\) is \(n^3 + 7\) one less than a multiple of 7? Type a set in curly brackets, for example {5, 6}; type {} if there are none.</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>The claim is only made for \(1 \le n \le 4\). What is the smallest positive integer \(n\) for which \(n^3 + 7\) is neither 1 more nor 1 less than a multiple of 7?</p>
<div>\(n =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>For each \(n\), find the multiple of 7 nearest to \(n^3 + 7\); it may be above or below.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>\(n\)</th><th>\(n^3 + 7\)</th><th>nearest multiple of 7</th><th>difference</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>8</td><td>7</td><td>\(+1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>15</td><td>14</td><td>\(+1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>34</td><td>35</td><td>\(-1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>71</td><td>70</td><td>\(+1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>132</td><td>133</td><td>\(-1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>223</td><td>224</td><td>\(-1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>350</td><td>350</td><td>\(0\)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<ol type="a">
<li><p>Only \(n = 3\) gives a number one less than a multiple of 7: \(34 = 35 - 1\). So the answer is \(\{3\}\).</p></li>
<li><p>For \(n = 1\) to 6 the claim holds, sometimes 1 above a multiple of 7 and sometimes 1 below. At \(n = 7\) it fails: 350 is itself a multiple of 7. Adding 7 does not change how far a number is from the nearest multiple of 7, so the claim is really about \(n^3\), and \(n^3\) is a multiple of 7 whenever \(n\) is.</p></li>
</ol>]]></text></generalfeedback>
